 :kotoba.lang.stdlib.manifest/revision
 {:version 6
  :date "2026-09-04"
  :kind :expansion
  :reason
  "A third artifact: stdlib.i64-set, the clojure.set algebra -- union,
   intersection, difference, subset?, equality, disjoint? -- written in
   Kotoba over a SORTED pair chain of i64, the collection shape
   stdlib.core already speaks. The language's typed-set owns the
   structure on every backend; what it does not own is the ALGEBRA, and
   the algebra is pure functions over two existing collections, so this
   is library work by the same split version 4 drew for sorted-map
   (Unison writes Map in Unison; this package writes Map and now Set in
   Kotoba). i64-only on a measured ground: the language has no string
   ORDER comparison (string=? only), so a sorted string set cannot even
   be constructed; when one exists, a string-set sibling is a version
   bump with its own oracle, not a widening of this module.

   MEASURED EN ROUTE, two refusals worth recording because the next
   module author hits both: (1) a recursive function whose arms are
   true/false but whose result type is INFERRED is refused with `if
   branches must have the same value type` -- the recursive call
   defaults to :i64 before inference closes the loop; the :bool result
   annotation on the defn fixes it (isolated: a two-line file with and
   without the annotation, exit 0 vs 65, amu check --jvm-free).
   (2) kir/execute refuses a STRING function name against the exports
   list -- the harness must pass quoted symbols ('t-subset), which the
   sorted-map oracle test already did and this one now matches.

   Verification: amu check --source-path lang --jvm-free exit 0;
   wasm32 compile exit 0 and instantiated through amu
   runtime/browser-host.mjs, golden main() = 42 (every export in the
   chain); edge vectors (dedup, negative items, disjoint-with-empty,
   subset-false, equal-false) main() = 42. JVM oracle:
   kotoba.lang.stdlib-i64-set-oracle-test replays 12 shape pairs
   (disjoint/overlapping/nested/identical/empty/duplicated) against
   clojure.set itself for all five operations, with validity asserted
   on every produced set; the suite moves 376 -> 383 tests with no new
   failures (the 13 failures + 1 error on origin/main, q1/q3, are
   unchanged -- measured on a clean checkout before and after)."
  :added-module :i64-set
  :added #{set-empty set-valid? set-from-chain set-contains?
           set-count set-to-chain set-union set-intersection
           set-difference set-subset? set-equal? set-disjoint?}
  :measured
  {:as-of "2026-09-04"
   :amu "a7e469f2"
   :kotoba-lang "b19e058"
   :route "amu check lang/conformance/stdlib/i64set.kotoba --source-path lang --jvm-free, exit 0; compiled to wasm32 and instantiated through amu runtime/browser-host.mjs, main() = 1111124113, which is the case's :expect"
   :oracle "clojure.set itself, in test/kotoba/lang/stdlib_i64_set_oracle_test.clj"}}

