   :records #{}
   :public-names
   #{sm-empty sm-get sm-contains? sm-assoc sm-dissoc sm-count
     sm-keys sm-vals sm-reduce sm-min-key sm-max-key}
   :private-helpers
   {sm-assoc #{node-height mk balance rotate-left rotate-right}
    sm-dissoc #{min-node}
    sm-keys #{keys-into}
    sm-vals #{vals-into}
    sm-reduce #{reduce-into}
    sm-max-key #{max-node}}
   ;; WHY AVL AND NOT LLRB. Depth, because depth is what recursion costs.
   ;; An AVL tree of n keys is at most 1.4405*log2(n+2) - 0.3277 levels deep;
   ;; a 2-3 left-leaning red-black tree is at most 2*log2(n+1). At 63 keys
   ;; that is 8 against 12, and each level is paid twice per insert -- once
   ;; descending and once rebalancing on the way back up.
   :structure
   {:kind :avl
    :node "(pair key (pair value (pair height (pair left right))))"
    :empty 0
    :rejected-alternatives
    {:record
     {:message "value type is outside the safe profile"
      :error-code :kotoba.error/value-type-outside-profile
      :exit 65
      :detail "(defrecord Node [k :i64 v :i64 l [:record :probe.rec/Node] r [:record :probe.rec/Node]]) -- a record field cannot name its own record. Measured 2026-09-02, amu ca869d79e, `amu check <file> --jvm-free`. Independently, there is no empty node to return: an empty record is refused (:kotoba.error/record-fields) and two nominal types on the arms of an if are refused with \"if branches must have the same value type\" (both already on record under :records)."}
     :variant
     {:message :no-such-head
      :detail "there is no variant-declaring head in lang/guest-grammar.edn to reach for."}
     :llrb
     {:reason :deeper
      :detail "correct, and 12 levels where AVL is 8 at n=63. Not measured in Kotoba; the arithmetic is the reason it was not written."}}}
   ;; Measured 2026-09-02 by test/kotoba/lang/stdlib_sorted_map_oracle_test.clj
   ;; (`what-ascending-inserts-cost`, which prints AVL-ASCENDING-HEIGHT and
   ;; DEFAULT-FUEL-512-ASCENDING-INSERTS).
   :costs
   {:budget "512 calls -- the KIR interpreter's default-fuel and the wasm host's per-instance fuel"
    :height {15 4, 31 5, 63 6}
    :avl-bound {15 5, 31 6, 63 8}
    :ascending-inserts-under-default-fuel 15
    :note "15 / 31 / 63 ASCENDING inserts -- the order that forces the most rotations -- produce trees 4, 5 and 6 levels deep, inside the AVL bound in all three cases. Building them is what does not fit: under the default budget of 512 calls the fifteenth ascending insert completes and the sixteenth traps on fuel, which is the same ceiling stdlib.core's sort-by has. Above it the function traps rather than answering wrongly. A LOOKUP is one call per level, so a 63-key tree is a 6-call get; it is the BUILD that is bounded, and a host that raises the budget raises it proportionally."
    :sm-count "O(n) -- the size is not stored in the node. A fifth pair cell on every node would buy back a walk only sm-count takes."}
   :internal-notes
   "Every public name is single-arity, as in :core, and sm-get carries its
    not-found sentinel as a third argument rather than returning an option:
    wasm32 still refuses to lower an if over option-some / option-none, and
    refuses the WHOLE project for it, reached or not (see :absent sm-get-option).
    Truth is 1 and 0, as it is for :core's every?, so a caller reading both
    modules reads one convention.
    sm-reduce takes a BINARY closure and hands it (pair key value); a ternary
    (invoke f acc k v) would need a three-argument closure, which nothing in
    this package anchors or has measured. A program with no binary `fn`
    literal of its own can anchor the dispatcher with
    stdlib.core/stdlib-binary-closure-anchor.
    sm-min-key and sm-max-key answer 0 for the empty map, and 0 is also a
    key; sm-count is the discriminator, and the oracle asserts the collision
    rather than choosing keys that avoid it."
   :status :frozen}
