  sm-reduce-with-a-ternary-closure
  {:reason :unanchored-arity
   :detail "(invoke f acc key value) would need a three-argument closure. stdlib.core anchors the binary dispatcher (stdlib-binary-closure-anchor) and nothing in this package has measured a ternary one, so sm-reduce hands its binary closure (pair key value) instead. Not a refusal that was seen -- an arity that was not measured, which is a different thing and is recorded as such."}
  sm-rank
  {:reason :not-attempted
   :detail "the size is not stored in the node (see the module's :costs), so there is no O(log n) rank or nth. Adding a fifth pair cell per node is a version bump with its own measurement, not a footnote to this one."}}

 ;; A recorded reason is a claim with a date on it, and the compiler moves
 ;; under it. Every :absent and :withdrawn entry was re-read on the date
 ;; below, against the toolchain named, and the verdict for each is here --
 ;; INCLUDING the ones that did not move, because "still true" and "nobody
 ;; looked" are otherwise the same silence.
 ;;
 ;; `stdlib-manifest-test` asserts that every :absent and :withdrawn name has
 ;; a verdict here, so a name added later cannot skip the re-read.
 :reasons-re-read
 {:as-of "2026-09-03"
  :toolchain "amu 57ba0ee0, with kotoba-sema 0a5dcc2b's src prepended to the classpath bin/amu computes -- the nbb entrypoint bin/amu uses for every wasm32 target, so no JDK took part. Each isolate is the one the entry itself records."
  :occasion "the map literal's VALUE type (kotoba-sema ADR 0033): a literal's value type was fixed at :i64 while the typed-map layer already carried :string, :bool, :keyword, record, nested-map, heterogeneous-vector and typed-set values. Two version-3 :absent entries had been recorded as MAP refusals, so all of them were re-read rather than only the ones that looked related."
  :verdicts
  {get-in :unchanged
   some :unchanged
   juxt :unchanged
   keep :unchanged
   sm-get-returning-an-option :unchanged
   sm-reduce-with-a-ternary-closure :unchanged
   sm-rank :unchanged
   ;; :withdrawn
   find :unchanged
   select-keys :unchanged
   group-by :unchanged
   update :stale
   option-some :unchanged option-none :unchanged option-some? :unchanged
   option-value :unchanged option-none? :unchanged
   ok :unchanged err :unchanged ok? :unchanged err? :unchanged
   unwrap-ok :unchanged unwrap-err :unchanged}
  :measured
  {get-in
   "NOT affected by the value widening, which is what prompted this re-read. The reason is monomorphism -- the path is a value in Clojure and part of the type here -- and a wider VALUE type does not give the language a way to type a lookup whose depth is a value. `[:map :i64 [:map :i64 :i64]]` was already constructible through typed-map-new; the literal `{1 (typed-map-new [:map :i64 :i64] 5 50)}` is new and changes nothing about get-in."
   some "reserved head; unchanged."
   juxt "arity in the name; unchanged."
   keep "a divergence note, not a refusal; unchanged."
   sm-get-returning-an-option
   "STILL REFUSED, re-measured on the entry's own isolate. `(defn f [x :i64] :option-i64 (if (> x 3) (option-some x) (option-none)))` passes `amu check` with exit 0 and `amu compile --target wasm32` answers {:error :wasm-typed-lowering, :code :kotoba/internal-error, \"unsupported typed Wasm expression\"}. The :returns-when stands."
   find "the same isolate as sm-get-returning-an-option, and the same refusal. Unchanged."
   sm-reduce-with-a-ternary-closure "an arity that was not measured, not a refusal that was seen; unchanged."
   sm-rank "not attempted; unchanged."
   select-keys "unchanged as recorded: the entry's reason is that it called `contains-key?`, which is not an operation in this language. `contains?` IS one as of 2026-09-03 (kotoba-sema ADR 0012) and compiles to wasm32 -- `(if (contains? {1 10} 1) 7 8)` exits 0 -- but that is a different head from the one the entry names, and rewriting select-keys onto it is an addition with an oracle and a version bump, not a correction to this record."
   group-by "unchanged as recorded, and for the same shape of reason as select-keys: the entry's message is the keyword-key refusal, which is closed, but this module's collection is a pair chain rather than a typed map, so writing group-by is an addition rather than the removal of an obstacle."
   update
   "STALE. The entry says `wasm32 refuses: a single file whose only function is (defn f [source :map key :keyword] :map (map-assoc source key 7)) exits 70`. Re-measured: that isolate compiles to wasm32 with exit 0, and the WHOLE recorded body -- (defn update [source :map key :keyword updater :i64] :map (map-assoc source key (invoke updater (map-get source key 0)))) -- compiles to wasm32-browser and answers 5 through amu runtime/browser-host.mjs, which is the value the entry itself predicts for the KIR backend. The :returns-when condition (`wasm32 can lower map-new / map-assoc`) is MET. Not owed to the value widening; recorded here because it was found by this re-read and a stale reason left standing is the defect this key exists to prevent. Adding the name back is a version bump with an oracle test, which this change does not make."
   :option-and-result-names
   "the eleven withdrawn option/result names (some?, option-some, option-none, option-some?, option-value, option-none?, ok, err, ok?, err?, unwrap-ok, unwrap-err) are withdrawn because the LANGUAGE owns those heads, which no compiler change moves. Unchanged."}}

 :records
 {:status :removed-in-version-2
  :was #{:Some :None :Ok :Err}
  :two-independent-reasons
  ["(defrecord None []) is refused: :kotoba.error/record-fields, 'record fields must be a non-empty unique bounded vector'. Isolated: a two-line file containing only (defrecord None []) and a main is refused the same way, and the same file with (defrecord Some [value]) is admitted."
   "even with None spelled to carry a field, a record-based Option is not expressible: Some and None are two distinct nominal types, so a function returning either is refused with 'if branches must have the same value type'. Measured on the file's own `find`."]
  :decision "the refusal of an empty record stays. See the workspace ADR named in lang/stdlib.edn."}

